<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cahokia Woodhenge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 900–<a href="/years/1100">1100</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Woodhenge is the name archaeologists give to a series of circles of large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> posts at Cahokia, near modern St Louis, built between about 900 and 1100. Excavated from the 1960s, the posts appear to have marked the solstices and equinoxes. A reconstruction stands at the Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cahokia-woodhenge.jpg" alt="Cahokia Woodhenge" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herb Roe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cahokia_Woodhenge_at_Sunrise_HRoe_2017sm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
